","key":true},{"title":"Budget Framework 2026-27 Report","url":"https://LBBD.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s179085/budget-framework-2026-27-report.pdf","committee":"Cabinet","date":"16/02/2026","key":true},{"title":"App D Statutory Budget Determination","url":"https://LBBD.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s179073/app-d-statutory-budget-determination.pdf","committee":"Cabinet","date":"16/02/2026","key":true},{"title":"App F - Budget Consultation","url":"https://LBBD.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s179075/app-f-budget-consultation.pdf","committee":"Cabinet","date":"16/02/2026","key":true},{"title":"2024-25 Draft Annual Governance Statement","url":"https://LBBD.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s178278/2024-25-draft-annual-governance-statement.pdf","committee":"Audit and Risk Committee","date":"14/01/2026","key":true},{"title":"Budget Scrutiny 2026-31 Cover Report","url":"https://LBBD.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s178419/budget-scrutiny-2026-31-cover-report.pdf","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","date":"12/01/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Full Council","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-06","committee":"Audit and Risk Committee","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-07","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-13","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-21","committee":"Health Scrutiny Committee","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-04","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-18","committee":"Full Council","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-22","committee":"Full Council","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Health Scrutiny Committee","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Pensions Committee","time":"7:00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Barking and Dagenham's pension fund underperforms investment targets","lead":"Barking and Dagenham's pension fund underperformed its investment targets across all time periods to June 2026, according to the council's Pensions Committee. The fund returned 7.28% in the quarter against a benchmark of 8.64%, 14.48% over 12 months against 16.60%, and 11.35% over three years against 12.82%. The committee's papers described the underperformance as material. Kempen Capital Management, the second-largest fund manager, returned 3.92% in the quarter against a benchmark of 13.03%. Hermes Infrastructure returned \u22121.80% over 12 months against a benchmark of 5.90%.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Pension Commitee Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s182370/182370.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Barking and Dagenham budget gap quadruples to \u00a327.9m as High Court judgment adds unbudgeted costs","lead":"Barking and Dagenham Council faces a budget shortfall of \u00a327.9m for 2027/28, more than four times the \u00a36.2m forecast only six months earlier, leaving it with reserves of just \u00a32.6m beyond its minimum safety cushion. The council's cabinet has acknowledged that its emergency fund of \u00a312m \u2014 less than 5 per cent of its annual spending \u2014 is now insufficient given the scale of financial pressures. The deterioration stems largely from \u00a322.1m in rising costs for adult social care, children's services and housing, plus an overspend crystallising from the current year. Compounding the crisis, a High Court judgment has ruled that the council must stop treating Universal Credit transitional protection as income in its Council Tax Support scheme, creating an unbudgeted annual cost of \u00a3150,000 and exposing three years of past awards to legal challenge. The council is setting aside \u00a3572,000 to compensate roughly 836 residents who received unlawful reductions in support, and must pause enforcement action against affected cases.","iso":"2026-09-15","paper_title":"CTS 2027-28 Report","paper_url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s182466/182466.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Pensions Committee","paper_title":"Pension Commitee Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s182370/182370.pdf","headline":"Hermes Infrastructure has delivered negative returns while benchmarks are positive, underperforming by 7.70% over 12 months","detail":"Infrastructure is allocated 3.77% of the fund. Hermes Infrastructure returned -1.80% over the 12 months to June against a 5.90% benchmark (underperformance of 7.70%), and -1.80% in Q2 against 1.44% benchmark (underperformance of 3.25%).","category":"governance","matters":"A fund manager delivering negative real returns when the benchmark is positive creates a sustained drag on overall returns and suggests either poor management or an unsuitable asset class allocation","figures":"-1.80% (12m and Q2) vs 5.90% and 1.44% benchmarks respectively; 3.77% allocation","quote":"Hermes Infrastructure 68,577,214 3.77 -1.80 -1.80 -0.63 4.23 6.15 LBBD03 5.9% Hurdle Rate 1.44 5.90 5.90 5.90 5.92 Excess Return -3.25 -7.70 -6.53 -1.67 0.22","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Pensions Committee","paper_title":"Pension Commitee Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s182370/182370.pdf","headline":"Kempen Capital Management, a major fund manager, significantly underperformed in Q2, returning only 3.92% against a benchmark of 13.03%","detail":"Kempen is allocated 16.08% of the fund (the second-largest allocation). Its Q2 return of 3.92% against a 13.03% benchmark represents a 9.10% underperformance, though its 12-month performance is better at 21.18% vs 25.28% benchmark (-4.10%).","category":"governance","matters":"A 9.10% Q2 shortfall on a 16% fund allocation represents a material drag on returns and raises questions about manager performance and suitability","figures":"Q2: 3.92% vs 13.03% benchmark (-9.10%); 12m: 21.18% vs 25.28% (-4.10%); 16.08% fund allocation","quote":"Kempen Capital Management 292,666,833 16.08 3.92 21.18 12.58 10.02 9.24 LBBD04 MSCI World ND 13.03 25.28 17.54 13.22 13.06 Excess Return -9.10 -4.10 -4.97 -3.20 -3.81","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Pensions Committee","paper_title":"Pension Commitee Performance Report 2026-27","paper_url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s182370/182370.pdf","headline":"The pension fund has underperformed its investment benchmark across all time periods","detail":"In the quarter to June, the fund returned 7.28% against a benchmark of 8.64% (shortfall of 1.36%). Over 12 months it returned 14.48% against 16.60% (shortfall of 2.12%). Over three years it returned 11.35% against 12.82% (shortfall of 1.47%). This persistent underperformance is material.","category":"governance","matters":"Consistent underperformance against the benchmarks that underpin the actuarial valuation increases the risk that employer contribution rates will need to rise at the next triennial revaluation, creating pressure on the council's revenue budget","figures":"Q2: 7.28% vs 8.64%; 12m: 14.48% vs 16.60%; 3y: 11.35% vs 12.82%","quote":"During the quarter the Fund returned 7.28% against the benchmark 8.64%, resulting in an underperformance of negative return of 1.36%","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Strategy 2027-28 Report","paper_url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s182474/182474.pdf","headline":"The Strategic Commercial Asset Management Delivery Framework (\u00a32bn capital investment, 60 schemes) is no longer financially viable under current interest rates and may require business case revision","detail":"The council is implementing a major regeneration and housing programme involving \u00a32bn of capital investment across 60 schemes, generating 2,000 residential units already built/in development and providing anticipated commercial income to support the budget. However, rising PWLB borrowing costs mean that new schemes no longer generate sufficient returns to cover long-term financing costs. The paper notes that while no changes are proposed to the currently-approved business case assumptions yet, officers will continue to monitor interest rates and may need to revise assumptions around tenure and grant.","category":"capital","matters":"This commercial framework is material to the council's financial plan and has been approved on specific return assumptions. If viability fails or the business case is revised downwards, this represents a significant hit to anticipated income and a material unplanned source of additional borrowing. It also demonstrates the council's exposure to interest rate risk on long-dated borrowing.","figures":"\u00a32bn capital investment, 60 schemes, 2,000 residential units built and 1,500 in development, \u00a32.8m additional funding requirement for asset management function","quote":"Given the significant rise in borrowing costs relative to the Council's historic Public Works Loan Board (PWLB) average, new schemes no longer generate sufficient returns to offset financing costs without revised assumptions around tenure and grant.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Strategy 2027-28 Report","paper_url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s182474/182474.pdf","headline":"General Fund reserves have depleted to critical levels with only \u00a32.6m available for emergencies beyond the minimum policy requirement","detail":"Usable reserves fell from \u00a3123.4m to \u00a3112.93m in one year. Of this, the General Fund reserve is \u00a314.59m, and council policy requires a minimum of \u00a312m. This leaves only \u00a32.6m available for other purposes. Additionally, the council now acknowledges that the \u00a312m minimum (representing less than 5% of net budget) is insufficient given current financial risks and uncertainty.","category":"reserves","matters":"Thin reserves provide minimal buffer for unexpected pressures or failed savings plans. The council's own S151 officer has flagged that reserve levels are no longer adequate for the scale of risks facing the organisation. This increases vulnerability to further shocks and could force emergency service restrictions or debt default if pressures materialise.","figures":"\u00a3123.37m \u2192 \u00a3112.93m reserves, \u00a314.59m General Fund, \u00a312m minimum required, \u00a32.6m available surplus, <5% of net budget","quote":"The Council's approved Reserve Policy requires a minimum of \u00a312m to be maintained in the General Fund reserve. Therefore, there is only \u00a32.6m that could be used for other purposes. The current minimum reserve balance of \u00a312m now represents less than 5% of the Council's net budget, which is considered insufficient given the scale of the financial risks facing the organisation.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Barking & Dagenham","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-20","since_iso":"2026-08-21","distress":false,"headline":"Hermes Infrastructure has delivered negative returns while benchmarks are positive, underperforming by 7.70% over 12 months","lead":"A fund manager delivering negative real returns when the benchmark is positive creates a sustained drag on overall returns and suggests either poor management or an unsuitable asset class allocation","also":["14 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Full Council on 2026-09-23","n_findings":15,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Pension Commitee Performance Report 2026-27","top_paper_url":"https://lbbd.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s182370/182370.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":131,"news_drafts_total":2}}